\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ a \\ 4 \end{pmatrix} \\
\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 4 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}Therefore, the dot product of these direction vectors must be equal to $0$. Recall that the dot product of two vectors $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ in $\mathbb{R}^{3}$ is given by:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{y} &= \|\mathbf{x}\| \|\mathbf{y}\| \cos\theta \\
&= \|\mathbf{x}\| \|\mathbf{y}\| \left( \frac{\mathbf{x} \cdot \mathbf{y}}{\|\mathbf{x}\| \|\mathbf{y}\|} \right) \\
